WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.900
[music]

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.460
I'm Emily Conover.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.340
I'm the physics writer for Science News.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.700
So the big discovery this past year was

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.650
the detection of gravitational waves

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:12.850
by the LIGO experiment.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.330
And we expect to see more from them

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.340
in the coming year.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.200
So that's really exciting.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.080
Everyone is super pumped

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.740
about gravitational waves right now.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.280
We could see more black holes coalescing

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.589
and creating waves that radiate out.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.380
The other thing LIGO might be able

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.340
to detect is merging neutron stars.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.380
So when these merge, they create gravitational waves

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.700
just like the merging black holes did.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.260
But one exciting thing about neutron stars is that

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.060
when they merge, they might create light that we could

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.540
see if we can locate where that collision happened

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.120
and point telescopes at it.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.380
Well particle physics is sort of in a weird state right now.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.740
It's a little bit of --

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.800
people are certain get anxious because

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.940
the Large Hadron Collider has found the Higgs Boson,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.420
but it hasn't found anything else.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.399
And we know that there's a lot of stuff

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.900
that's still unexplained in particle physics,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.840
like we don't know what dark matter is --

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.200
the stuff that makes up a large portion of

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.680
the matter in the universe.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.480
So scientists are starting to look in some

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.480
more out-of-the-way corners for new physics.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.240
So one of these experiments is

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.310
called the muon g-2 experiment.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.000
That's going to start taking data in 2017.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.340
So in this experiment they look at muons,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.060
which are like heavy electrons.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.360
They send these muons through a magnet,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.360
and they look at the magnetic properties of the muon

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.920
and try to understand those.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.180
The muon behaves like a little bar magnet,

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.000
like it has a North and a South Pole.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.280
And that magnet, when it's in the magnetic field,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.820
rotates around. It precesses, kind of like a top.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.540
So what the muon g-2 experiment is going to do

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.900
is they're going to measure the rate of that precession.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.780
That can tell you all kinds of things

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.420
about physics that we might not know yet,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.770
we may not understand yet,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.080
because the rate of this precession

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:55.500
depends on all these little particles

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.300
that are blipping in and out of space

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.780
kind of in the quantum vacuum all the time.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.960
So if the number that we measure

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.980
differs from the prediction,

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.360
that means that there could be another particle

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.780
out there that we aren't aware of.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.520
Another new experiment kicking off in 2017

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:13.480
is something called the Cold Atom Laboratory.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.360
They're taking a weird quantum state of matter

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.760
called a Bose-Einstein condensate,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.980
and they're sending it into space.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.000
So a Bose-Einstein condensate is state of matter

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.540
that you get when you make atoms

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:24.660
really, really, really cold.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.600
And they all sort of take on the same quantum state.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.780
So they all kind of behave as one,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:32.100
and it's sort of like they all become one with The Borg.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.860
Physicists treat these Bose-Einstein condensates

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.120
like a playground,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.580
They do everything from studying

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.170
the fundamental laws of physics to

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.480
simulating the early universe or black holes.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.080
The reason they want to send this to space

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.000
is because you have to get it really cold,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
and in space you can you can get it even colder.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.820
And you don't have to worry about

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.300
the effect of gravity pulling on your atoms

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.420
because you're in free fall

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.900
at the International Space Station.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.000
This will be the coldest place in the universe

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.300
as far as we know.

